A||||17||Julian||T-top question.||||5||
Z||000000||Julian||02-24-2001||09:44 AM||||I need a little info on t-tops.<BR>Is it true that they were NOT factory installed? In other words an aftermarket dealer option. Reason I'm asking is I know there is a t-top regestry just like there is a Mera registry & Mera's were done aftermarket. (aftermarket may not be the right word....you know what I mean... third party after it came off the assembly line).<P>Well the only option my Mera is missing is the T-top (wonder who the cheap guy was who ordered it like that!!!!!).  I see t-top kits for sale & wonder if they are real Fiero kits or just replicas.<P>.....someone please clear me up.....<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/meralogo.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||T-top Fiero||02-24-2001||10:32 AM||||Only the 88 Fieros could be ordered from the dealer with the T-top option. The 88's ordered with T-tops were shipped from the factory to a company called C&C, which did the installation. Then the cars were shipped to the dealer which had ordered the conversion. These cars have the option code CJB on the RPO sticker. All other T-top Fieros were aftermarket installations by C&C or one of their dealers.<BR>As far as I know C&C was the only company to offer a T-top kit for the Fiero, so if you see a T-top kit for sale it can be considered the real thing.<P>Hope that helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||RossT||02-24-2001||11:43 AM||||Those that were ordered from a dealer or from and individual got the CJB code as one of the RPO codes.  The cars were shipped from Pontiac Michigan to Brighton Michigan where the t-tops were installed by C&C then shipped of to the dealer.<P>The orginal window sticker was not coded for the t-tops.  That was placed on a secondary sticker next to the orginal window sticker.<P>There were many C&C franchises thru out the country that installed the t-tops POST-PRODUCTION.  Many times a dealer would have it done in an attempt to sell the car.  Or the Onwer could take the car to C&C to have it done.  These cars do not have the CJB code.<P>The fiero t-top registry follows those cars that were pre-ordered (had CJB) with the t-tops.  <P>If you want t-tops I suggest you try to find a kit soon. There are not many left and replacement parts are hard to find and most likely will not be re-produced (except for the p-seals).<P>Contact <A HREF="http://www.fieroparts.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroparts.com</A>  for more info on parts.<P>I personally think that t-tops on a Mera would be awesome!!!<P>expcet to pay $1500-2500 for a kit (depends on where you find one) and then another $1000 to have it installed.<P>someone correct me if any of my info is wrong<P>There  is nothing like a hot summer day with the tops out!!!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||02-24-2001||12:09 PM||||Creative Concepts (C&C) I think was the name is a company in Detroit that does a lot of after final assembly modifications for manufacturer. Mustang and LeBaron convertibles, and lots of different T-tops.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fieroparts.com||02-24-2001||12:24 PM||||RossT,<P>The window sticker did show the CJB option<BR>but no price, as you said on a secondary sticker with the price. <BR>Do you have a pics of your car?<BR>Love to see it!<P>"The orginal window sticker was not coded for the t-tops.  That was placed on a secondary sticker next to the orginal window sticker."<P><P>------------------<BR>1988 GT  000117.1 <BR>Miles T-Tops <BR>Loaded med. red<BR>AS NEW AS THEY GET!<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroparts.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroparts.com</A>  <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fieroparts.com/88gt/88gt117-46.jpg">      <P>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fieroparts.com/mods/88coupe3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||RossT||02-24-2001||02:58 PM||||T-tops in bright red.  Sadly no CJB, but still looks nice. <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image020_1.jpg">   <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image005.jpg">   <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image019_1.jpg">    <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image002_3.jpg">   <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image017_1.jpg">   <BR> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ivoice2_1.JPG"> <P><BR> You are correct about the coded window sticker with no pricing.  Looks kind of strange<P>CJB...................................no cost<P>then on the second sticker the cost is added on.<P>That medium red metallic is awesome on your car...<P>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 02-24-2001).]<P>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 02-24-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 02-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfiero||02-24-2001||05:09 PM||||I don't know who Creative Concepts are, but the company who developed the T-Top kits for the Fiero was called Cars & Concepts (hence, C&C).<P>They were later known as Farmont Skylite.<P>The cost for an aftermarket (non CJB) installation in 1989 was about $1100.  The "kit" was $500 and the installation $600 and some change.  I bought an '85 SE several years ago from the original owners who converted it to T-Tops in Feb. 1989.  I don't have the car anymore, but I still have the receipt.<P>The only real difference between an aftermarket installation and a factory installation is the CJB code on the RPO sticker.  The weatherseals on the factory (ship through) are different from the aftermarket kits and the trim caps on the factory installs are molded the proper color, while the kits were all light gray and needed to be painted to match the interior color.<P>I just found the receipt.....I was a little off.  The kit was $600 and the labor to install it was $495......add $43.20 for tax and the grand total was $1138.20.   <p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 02-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||T-top Fiero||02-24-2001||08:40 PM||||I guess I was lucky when I aquired my T-tops. I got the whole T-top setup out of a junkyard car for $50. After buying some new trim caps ($8 each) and P seals ($50 each) I did my own installation. I am very happy the way it turned out.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mycar1.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mycar2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||rodmcneill||02-24-2001||09:09 PM||||T-top Fiero, nice installation and one helluva good deal on the kit.||||reg||14
Z||000009||Huck||02-26-2001||01:47 AM||||I have an 87 GT with T-tops... I just bought the car in August 2000, and they started leaking shortly afterwards (passenger side only though) ..  However, there is also a considerable amount of air noise (like driving with the window open  1/8 of an inch).  After looking into it, it appears the glass portion accross the front of each side does not contact the seals at all.  Almost as if the middle T where the glass slides into is up too high.  I tried to shim them down a little, without any luck.  If the stereo is loud, you dont hear it, but when someone wants to go for a tour in my car, its somewhat annoying and embarassing.  <P>What would be my best bet in fixing this problem? I already have to order seals to fix my leak (had better do both sides while Im at it), but how would I possibly lower the T itself (if thats what should be done), or can I raise the seals up to connect with the glass?  <P>I'm more of a Fiero enthusiast which enjoys the style and handling of the car than a do-it-yourself-er, though I always give something a try before I pay someone else to do it!!<P>When it gets nicer out I'll take some pic's to show exactly what my problem is.<P>thanks.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||fieroparts.com||02-26-2001||09:31 AM||||Sounds like you need P-seals <A HREF="http://www.fieroparts.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroparts.com</A> <BR>1-800-255-3437<BR>207-934-0103||||reg||1
Z||000011||Raydar||02-26-2001||02:17 PM||||Huck,<BR>There's also an adjustment for the steel latch pin. You'll have to take the plastic cover off of the latch mechanism. Once you have the cover off, you will see two screws that hold a plate, where the pin comes out of the mechanism. Loosen the two screws just a little, and move the plate (and the pin) toward the glass just a bit. Then tighten the screws. This will cause the glass to be pulled down a little tighter to the seals. Don't over-do it, though. The pins can put too much pressure on the latch plates, and tear the "holes" out. <BR>Of course, if the center strip really is too high this won't help, but most of my wind noise came from the outside front corner of my right panel. <P>Check this link for useful info... <A HREF="http://www.fierottop.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierottop.com</A> <BR>Follow the "waterleak manual" link.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000012||RossT||02-26-2001||04:51 PM||||Huck, you may also have to adjust the travel of the glass up 1/8 inch so that it touches the glass weatherstrip.  The link that radar gave you tells you how to do it.  (not fun).||||reg||1
Z||000013||ChopTop||02-26-2001||09:52 PM||||I purchased a 85GT in late summer of 1988.  I live in Michigan and the C&C factory was about an hour away.  I called them in the spring of 1990 and inquired if they had any kits left.  They told me they had 10 kits left and the cost would be $900, which included installation.  I made an appointment and dropped my car off to them two weeks later.  When I picked my car up they told me they only had 3 sets left and once they were gone, they were gone.  I was told this was by service technicians.  I still have the car, t-tops have never leaked but I do have a little wind noise around the drivers side window.||||reg||1
Z||000014||fieroparts.com||02-26-2001||09:58 PM||||choptop,<P>Is your car a 1988 Silver GT?||||reg||14
